ent after its grading checks. These instructions do not open a form or release an unvalidated assessment.\n"},{"key":"CML3113|ML_PROJECT","track":"IUEA","cohort":"CML3113","id":"ML_PROJECT","label":"ML_PROJECT","week":15,"title":"Engineering ML project","maximum":100,"component":"ml_project","formats":["ipynb","pdf","docx"],"opens_at":"2026-11-27T08:00:00+03:00","closes_at":"2026-12-18T08:00:00+03:00","preserve_existing":false,"grading":"rubric","brief":"# CML3113 ML_PROJECT — Engineering ML project\n\n## Start here\n\nUse your own Student Registration Number exactly as on your university record. Sign in to upload with the Google account registered with the lecturer. If you need account or file help, read STUDENT_HOW_TO.md supplied with the student materials. A repository is a folder of saved project files; an upload form is where you hand in assessed work. Saving to a repository does not submit an assignment.\n\nOpen ML_PROJECT_student.ipynb as the assignment notebook. Keep these input/helper files in its runtime folder: engineering_data.csv. In Colab use File > Open notebook > Upload for .ipynb; use the Files sidebar Upload button for data and .py helpers. Save your own notebook copy in Drive. Keep the issued raw files unchanged.\n\n## Do the work\n\n1. Read the task text above each answer cell before editing. Complete the supplied notebook rather than starting an unrelated notebook.\n2. Replace YOUR_ID in STUDENT_ID with your own identity, keeping the quotes. Check COHORT. Keep every required variable/function name unchanged.\n3. Write code in the answer cells. A function must return the requested result; printing a result alone does not return it. Follow the specified input types, output types, units and missing-value rules.\n4. Run cells from top to bottom. A blank answer can fail a visible check or raise NotImplementedError until you complete it. Read the first error and fix its cause. Never change helper files or tests to force a pass.\n5. Where present, double-click the text cell headed My written evidence and write your own explanations, keeping that heading. Read every written criterion; copied task instructions or code comments do not replace your reasoning.\n6. Restart the session and Run all. Upload missing input files again if the temporary runtime has lost them. Inspect the results, download the finished .ipynb and reopen it to check that you saved the final version.\n\nRead TASKS.md and complete ALL its rubric criteria as well as the named functions. Frame the prediction problem, document data and features, separate the equipment groups, compare the baseline and two fitted models, report validation evidence and limitations, and write the two-page engineering brief. Write your report in DOCX or selectable-text PDF. Include contribution and AI-use declarations where required, a repository URL and run instructions. Keep README, requirements.txt and source data in the repository; put concise relevant run/evidence text in the uploaded report too, because a link alone cannot be graded.\n\nCreate a blank document in Word or Google Docs. Put your identity, cohort and assessment title at the top. Use the headings requested below, explain your decisions in your own words, and give units beside numerical results. In Google Docs choose File > Download > Microsoft Word (.docx) or PDF Document (.pdf). In Word use Save As for DOCX or Export for PDF. Reopen the downloaded file; check that text can be selected and charts have readable captions. A scan, photo or image-only PDF is not a readable report for automated assessment.\n\n## Check and submit\n\nSubmit ONE completed notebook AND ONE complete DOCX or selectable-text PDF report together. Keep all required written sections in that report. Use your own identity in the filename, with slashes changed to underscores for IUEA, followed by _ML_PROJECT.ipynb. Do not submit the literal name STUDENT_ID. Do not upload guided_practice.ipynb, helper files, the ZIP or raw data unless the brief specifically requests them.\n\n## How to hand in\n\nOpen the correct cohort/assessment form below and read its current deadline. Fill the required identity fields; leave Team ID blank for individual work. Choose Add file > Upload and select the complete required file set. Wait for the filenames to appear, then click Submit. Keep the confirmation page and local copies. Each file must be at most 10 MB. If replacing work before the deadline, send the complete set again; the latest on-time response replaces the earlier response. If the form is closed or upload fails, keep your files and contact the lecturer; do not use a different assignment form.\n\nStudent submission link: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe_nQBvitfOInzsUcXkNH9V5Ykc95ymXHHeujjqHXX2lNzgpg/viewform\n\nPublished closing time (UTC+03:00 is Kampala): 2026-12-18T08:00:00+03:00. Read the live form for any announced revision.\n\nPreparation guidance only until the lecturer releases this assessment after its grading checks. These instructions do not open a form or release an unvalidated assessment.\n"},{"key":"CML3113|DEMO","track":"IUEA","cohort":"CML3113","id":"DEMO","label":"DEMO","week":15,"title":"Presentation and individual reflection","maximum":100,"component":"demo","formats":["pptx","pdf","docx"],"opens_at":"2026-11-27T08:00:00+03:00","closes_at":"2026-12-18T11:00:00+03:00","preserve_existing":false,"grading":"rubric","brief":"# CML3113 DEMO — Presentation and individual reflection\n\n## Start here\n\nUse your own Student Registration Number exactly as on your university record. Sign in to upload with the Google account registered with the lecturer. If you need account or file help, read STUDENT_HOW_TO.md supplied with the student materials. A repository is a folder of saved project files; an upload form is where you hand in assessed work. Saving to a repository does not submit an assignment.\n\n## Do the work\n\n1. In PowerPoint choose Blank Presentation, or open https://slides.google.